eck), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Alabanda was a city of ancient Caria [S1]; it took back the name Alabanda [S2]; this happened after the Battle of Magnesia [S2]; the battle was in 190 BC [S2]; the remains of a theatre survive among its ruins today [S3]; excavations have yielded very few inscriptions [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-02: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-02-verify-024 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Alabanda was a city of ancient Caria (Anatolia; the site is near Doğanyurt, Çine, Aydın Province, Turkey) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabanda]; The city had earlier lost its name, being renamed Antiochia of the Chrysaorians under the Seleucids [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabanda]; The city''s name reverted to Alabanda (it took back its name) after the Seleucid defeat at the Battle of Magnesia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabanda]; The Battle of Magnesia was in 190 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabanda]; Ruins of Alabanda survive today, west of Çine [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabanda]; Today the remains of a theatre survive among the ruins of Alabanda [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabanda]; Excavations at Alabanda have yielded very few inscriptions [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabanda]"}]'::jsonb),
